1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,491
This is a YOLO face model running on my
laptop's GPU,

2
00:00:03,491 --> 00:00:05,570
at about ten milliseconds a frame.

3
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:10,825
The same scan on the CPU takes twenty-
eight milliseconds a frame,

4
00:00:10,825 --> 00:00:12,090
and the queue fills up.

5
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:18,882
Three models looked at the same frame and
reported twenty-two, two and six faces,

6
00:00:18,882 --> 00:00:23,190
and since there's no ground truth here,
more boxes doesn't mean more correct.

7
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:30,873
Everything runs on the PC, with a model
file from Hugging Face,

8
00:00:30,873 --> 00:00:35,155
ONNX Runtime inside the C# app, and the
GPU through DirectML,

9
00:00:35,155 --> 00:00:37,050
with the CPU as a fallback.

10
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:44,409
Each 4K frame is shrunk to six-forty by
six-forty without stretching the faces,

11
00:00:44,409 --> 00:00:49,619
then from the thousands of candidates the
model returns, I keep the confident ones,

12
00:00:49,619 --> 00:00:53,950
map them back onto the 4K frame, and drop
the overlapping duplicates.

13
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:00,956
On a 1080p frame that's twenty-three
milliseconds

14
00:01:00,956 --> 00:01:03,245
on the CPU and under five on the

15
00:01:03,245 --> 00:01:06,250
RTX, but at 4K, the decoder sets the pace.

16
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:13,244
One bug never shipped. This YOLO26 model
was exported without NMS,

17
00:01:13,244 --> 00:01:17,959
so my classic decoder would have read its
corners as centres and drawn wrong boxes

18
00:01:17,959 --> 00:01:18,763
with no error,

19
00:01:18,763 --> 00:01:23,305
which is why each model family now has a
profile that checks the output when it

20
00:01:23,305 --> 00:01:23,650
loads.

21
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:28,994
The rest of the app only sees

22
00:01:28,994 --> 00:01:31,709
one interface, because I'm not grading
models here,

23
00:01:31,709 --> 00:01:35,010
I'm building the app so that a better
model can plug in later.

24
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:48,283
I scan five frames a second rather than
all sixty,

25
00:01:48,283 --> 00:01:50,210
skip the frames in between without

26
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:52,873
converting their pixels, and reuse the
buffers,

27
00:01:52,873 --> 00:01:54,970
so the garbage collector stays quiet.

28
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:04,530
So that's the whole path, from the decoder
through to the stored boxes.

29
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:12,450
And the boxes are blurred live while the
video plays.

30
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:17,650
Now, part two, turning detections into
people.

31
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:22,130
Each face keeps its track number from one
scan to the next.

32
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:27,402
Detections become tracks, and tracks can
become people,

33
00:02:27,402 --> 00:02:31,230
with the tracker linking each face from
one scan to the next.

34
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:40,389
Between scans, holding each box still let
a moving face walk right out of it,

35
00:02:40,389 --> 00:02:42,580
so now the box slides along its track,

36
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:44,771
and the share of boxes more than eight

37
00:02:44,771 --> 00:02:47,308
pixels off the face fell from about
eighteen

38
00:02:47,308 --> 00:02:48,230
percent to four.

39
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:58,371
Small, fast faces kept breaking tracks,
because every scan started a new one,

40
00:02:58,371 --> 00:03:00,380
until a distance fallback fixed it,

41
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:02,504
taking one minute of footage from two

42
00:03:02,504 --> 00:03:05,030
hundred and sixty-two tracks down to
eighty.

43
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:12,452
Track numbers never repeat, even after a
re-scan,

44
00:03:12,452 --> 00:03:16,690
because otherwise a new face could inherit
the number of a face someone had already

45
00:03:16,690 --> 00:03:17,150
approved.

46
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:29,150
Grouping faces into people is opt-in, and
it runs entirely on the device.

47
00:03:34,050 --> 00:03:37,501
It takes three sharp shots per track,
aligns them on five landmarks,

48
00:03:37,501 --> 00:03:40,141
and turns each into five hundred and
twelve numbers,

49
00:03:40,141 --> 00:03:42,323
so close numbers become a suggested
person,

50
00:03:42,323 --> 00:03:46,130
though the app never groups two tracks
from the same frame into one person.

51
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:55,007
I rename Thom, the person on screen, and
then Celia,

52
00:03:55,007 --> 00:03:57,794
and both names carry over to all of their
tracks,

53
00:03:57,794 --> 00:04:00,410
even in a scene from much earlier in the
film.

54
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:07,029
Grouping can miss a track, like this one
of Thom in profile,

55
00:04:07,029 --> 00:04:09,788
so I pick his name under Merge into, click
Merge,

56
00:04:09,788 --> 00:04:12,210
and the label on the video changes to
Thom.

57
00:04:17,950 --> 00:04:23,403
And every edit can be undone, because each
one makes a new immutable snapshot,

58
00:04:23,403 --> 00:04:25,430
so undo simply goes back one.

59
00:04:29,950 --> 00:04:33,046
And if the AI missed someone, I draw a
box,

60
00:04:33,046 --> 00:04:36,070
and OpenCV's tracker follows them forward.

61
00:04:40,050 --> 00:04:43,210
That's the whole path, from a track to a
person.

62
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:52,358
The costs are that DirectML is in
maintenance mode,

63
00:04:52,358 --> 00:04:55,602
the YOLO models come with Ultralytics'
AGPL terms,

64
00:04:55,602 --> 00:04:58,586
the face-embedding model is for research
only,

65
00:04:58,586 --> 00:05:01,570
and the tracker can swap two faces that
cross.

66
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:11,250
Next comes fail-safe review, and a
parallel 4K export pipeline.

